loh@fraser.sfu.ca (Keith Meng-Wei Loh) writes:

>1) Luck:  how can you tell how lucky you are? 

You can't. If you mean the accurate value of luck. If you quaf a potion
of enlightenment, it may tell you you are lucky or extremely lucky or
something, but there's no way knowing it better than that.

>2) Prayer: how can you tell when your prayer times out so you can pray 
>again? 

Well.. if you sacrifice something and see a four-leaf clover, you can be
sure the timeout has expired. Or if the gods seem mollified.
Again, I don't think there is any other way to exactly know when it times
out. Of course, the longer it has been since you prayed or your god gave
you something, the more likely it is to have been timed out.. if you are
NOT Hand of Elbereth/Envoy of Balance/Glory of Arioch and it has been
more than 1000 turns ago and the last time the god was satisfied with you,
you can be sure..

>3) Wishing: I got to wish once when, as a Valkyrie, I freed the water
>demon from a fountain. This surprised me, the Water Demon usually pounds
>my head in. So, why didn't it this time and how can I duplicate this
>cool experience (I wished for Mjollnir as it said to do on the wish list)?

That's random, and your luck has probably something to do with it.. it
MAY happen, but it doesn't happen often.

>4) How do you get lucky? 

Some of the things you may do to get lucky:

- sacrifice fresh corpses on the altar of your own god (DON'T sacrifice 
  humans or werecreatures unless you are chaotic, and DON'T sacrifice elves
  if you are an elf yourself. DON't sacrifice co-aligned unicorns)
- throw gems to unicorns (don't throw identified valuable gems to non-coaligned
  ones, though)
- give money to priests
- carry a blessed luckstone (an uncursed one also gives you luck, but it 
  also prevents any kind of luck timeouts, a blessed one only prevents good
  luck from timeouts)

There are more things to do, but these are the most obvious ones and the ones
I remember without trying to find any spoiler texts :-)
